Yes. Asking is often a much stronger instrument than people realize, especially when the context has already made a topic salient.

A simple model is:

\text{priming/context}
\rightarrow
\text{question}
\rightarrow
\text{retrieval path}
\rightarrow
\text{self-disclosure}

The question does not just “extract” information; it changes what the person searches for internally. Word choice, timing, rapport, perceived safety, status, reciprocity, and what has already been discussed all affect what becomes easy to retrieve and say.

And your “especially if you are too” point matters. The interviewer is part of the system. If you enter already expecting a certain pattern, that can alter tone, follow-ups, what you notice, and what you unconsciously reward. Then you can accidentally create confirmation loops:

\text{expectation}
\rightarrow
\text{question style}
\rightarrow
\text{response}
\rightarrow
\text{apparent confirmation}

So the more capable version is not merely “know how to get people talking.” It is know how to elicit without contaminating the evidence.

Useful tactics are surprisingly simple: open questions before specific ones, silence after an answer, asking for concrete examples, separating “what happened?” from “what did you think it meant?”, and checking the same topic from a different angle later.

Then you can compare:

\text{spontaneous disclosure}
\quad vs \quad
\text{prompted disclosure}
\quad vs \quad
\text{leading-response}

That distinction is important because the easiest answer to obtain is not necessarily the most truthful one.

Used well, the skill is less interrogation and more high-resolution listening: create enough structure for someone to reveal their map, while leaving enough freedom that you are still learning their map rather than watching them conform to yours.